id: log_platform_specific
label: Log Platform-Specific Implementation
package: platform_core
description: >
  Platform specific calls related to the timestamp source for the logger, plus
  the pre-sleep and post-sleep handlers. The timestamp source is selected by
  silicon series:
  .
  On Series 3 the timestamp source depends on whether RAIL is present. With RAIL
  present the radio PROTIMER (protocol timer) is used via direct register access
  (the logger declares just the PROTIMER registers it reads in sl_log_protimer.h,
  at their fixed offsets that are common across Series 3, since the public device
  package omits radio peripheral headers): each log event carries
  a 64-bit time where LBASECNT is the raw 32-bit timestamp and LWRAPCNT is the
  32-bit hardware epoch, read atomically via an LPRECNT latch. Without RAIL the radio
  power domain that hosts the PROTIMER is not enabled, so a general-purpose
  TIMERn is used instead - the same model as Series 2.
  .
  On Series 2 (and Series 3 without RAIL) a general-purpose TIMERn is used
  through the timer HAL, providing a free-running raw-counter timestamp plus a
  software epoch that together form a 64-bit monotonic time.
  .
  When the power_manager service is also present in the project, this
  component additionally compiles the EM-transition glue: it subscribes
  to power_manager events and suspends the logger across EM2/EM3. No
  source from that path is built when power_manager is absent.
  .
  Deep-sleep timestamp continuity: when RAIL is present on Series 3 (detected via
  SL_CATALOG_RAIL_LIB_PRESENT from the rail_lib component) it already keeps the
  shared PROTIMER synchronized to the low-frequency RTC across EM2/EM3, so the
  logger reads the PROTIMER directly and applies no sleep compensation of its
  own. Otherwise (Series 2, or Series 3 without RAIL), the logger back-fills the
  time with elapsed sleep time read from sleeptimer (which abstracts whatever
  low-frequency RTC the target SoC uses).
category: Services|Log
quality: production
metadata:
  sbom:
    license: Zlib
provides:
- name: log_platform_core
requires:
  # General-purpose TIMER timestamp source. Used on Series 2 always, and on
  # Series 3 only when RAIL is absent (with RAIL present the radio PROTIMER is
  # the source). The radio PROTIMER cannot be reached without RAIL's radio
  # clock/power bring-up, so the no-RAIL Series 3 path falls back to a TIMERn -
  # the same model as Series 2.
- name: hal_timer
  condition: [device_series_2, device_has_timer]
- name: hal_timer
  condition: [device_series_3, device_has_timer]
- name: device_peripheral
  condition: [device_series_2, device_efx]
- name: device_peripheral
  condition: [device_series_3, device_efx]
- name: clock_manager_runtime
  condition: [device_efx]
- name: interrupt_manager
  condition: [device_series_2, device_efx, device_has_timer]
- name: interrupt_manager
  condition: [device_series_3, device_efx, device_has_timer]
- name: sleeptimer
  condition: [power_manager]
root_path: platform_core/platform/service/sl_log/
source:
- path: src/sl_log_platform_specific_s2.c
  condition: [device_series_2]
- path: src/sl_log_platform_specific_s3.c
  condition: [device_series_3]
  # Shared general-purpose TIMERn timestamp source. Compiled alongside each
  # series file; its body compiles out on the Series 3 + RAIL PROTIMER path
  # (SLI_LOG_USE_PROTIMER == 1).
- path: src/sli_log_timer_timestamp.c
  condition: [device_series_2]
- path: src/sli_log_timer_timestamp.c
  condition: [device_series_3]
- path: src/sl_log_power_manager.c
  condition: [power_manager]
include:
- path: inc
  file_list:
  - path: sli_log_timer_timestamp.h
- path: inc
  condition: [power_manager]
  file_list:
  - path: sl_log_power_manager.h
  # Per-series inlined timestamp accessors. The logger core (sl_log.c) includes
  # the fixed name "sl_log_hal_inline.h"; the matching series directory is put
  # on the include path so the right implementation is selected at compile time
  # (no function-pointer indirection on the event-stamping hot path).
- path: inc/s2
  condition: [device_series_2]
  file_list:
  - path: sl_log_hal_inline.h
- path: inc/s3
  condition: [device_series_3]
  file_list:
  - path: sl_log_hal_inline.h
  - path: sl_log_protimer.h
template_contribution:
- name: event_handler
  condition: [power_manager]
  value:
    event: service_init
    include: sl_log_power_manager.h
    handler: sli_log_pm_subscribe
  priority: 9999
  # Series 3 + RAIL only: derive the PROTIMER tick rate, which is what switches
  # timestamping on for every reader. It cannot happen in the logger's own init -
  # that runs at service_init, before RAIL's stack_init handler brings the
  # PROTIMER up and makes its registers reachable - and it cannot happen on the
  # logging hot path, whose inlined accessors deliberately never touch the
  # peripheral. Ordered last in stack_init so RAIL (priority -9000) has run.
- name: event_handler
  condition: [device_series_3, rail_lib]
  value:
    event: stack_init
    include: sli_log_timer_timestamp.h
    handler: sli_log_protimer_prime_timebase
  priority: 9999
ui_hints:
  visibility: never
